Process Synchronization

This topic answers a very important question:

What happens when multiple processes or threads try to access the same data at the same time?

First Understand the Problem

Suppose a bank account contains:

Balance = ₹1000

Two ATM transactions happen simultaneously.

Thread 1

Withdraw:

₹100
Thread 2

Withdraw:

₹100

Expected Balance:

1000 - 100 - 100
= ₹800

But something strange can happen.

What Actually Happens?

Thread 1 reads:

Balance = 1000

Before Thread 1 updates it,

Thread 2 also reads:

Balance = 1000

Thread 1 writes:

Balance = 900

Thread 2 writes:

Balance = 900

Final Balance:

₹900

Wrong!

Expected:

₹800
Why Did This Happen?

Because both threads accessed shared data simultaneously.

This problem is called:

Race Condition
Race Condition
Definition

A race condition occurs when multiple processes or threads access and modify shared data simultaneously, causing unpredictable results.

Easy Definition

The result depends on:

Who executes first?
Who executes faster?

Hence the name:

Race
Real-Life Example

