Critical Section

Most Important Synchronization Concept.

Suppose code:

balance = balance - 100;

looks like one statement.

Actually CPU performs:

Read balance
Modify balance
Write balance

This part accesses shared data.

Called:

Critical Section
Definition

A critical section is a part of a program where shared resources are accessed.

Example:

balance = balance - 100;

Critical section.

Why Is Critical Section Dangerous?

Because multiple threads entering simultaneously can corrupt data.

Example:

Thread 1 enters
Thread 2 enters

at the same time.

Data becomes inconsistent.

Goal of Synchronization

Ensure:

Only one thread/process
enters critical section
at a time.
Critical Section Problem

OS designers wanted:

Mutual Exclusion

Only one thread in critical section.

Progress

If nobody is inside, someone should be allowed to enter.

Bounded Waiting

Nobody should wait forever.

These three conditions form the famous:

Critical Section Requirements
1. Mutual Exclusion

Most important.

Rule:

At most one thread
inside critical section.

Example:

Bathroom with one lock.

If Rahul enters:

Mohit waits.
2. Progress

If critical section is empty:

Someone should be allowed in.

Bad system:

Nobody inside
Everyone waiting

Makes no sense.

3. Bounded Waiting

A thread should not wait forever.

Example:

Rahul keeps entering.

Mohit never gets chance.

Bad system.

